1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,810
In Eswatini, one king still chooses the
prime minister.

2
00:00:04,310 --> 00:00:07,697
He shares the throne with his mother, and
together they rule a

3
00:00:07,717 --> 00:00:09,475
country smaller than New Jersey.

4
00:00:09,975 --> 00:00:13,178
Until twenty eighteen, you would have
known it by another name.

5
00:00:13,678 --> 00:00:15,312
Then the king changed it.

6
00:00:15,812 --> 00:00:19,913
In the next four minutes: why he changed
it, how one king erased the

7
00:00:19,933 --> 00:00:24,035
constitution, and a ceremony where
thousands of women carry reeds to

8
00:00:24,055 --> 00:00:25,085
the queen mother.

9
00:00:25,585 --> 00:00:27,585
But first, where is this place?

10
00:00:28,085 --> 00:00:32,935
Eswatini is landlocked, tucked between
South Africa and Mozambique.

11
00:00:33,435 --> 00:00:35,680
About one point two million people liv
here.

12
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:38,954
They are the Swazi, and they speak
siSwati.

13
00:00:39,454 --> 00:00:40,992
The name comes from a king.

14
00:00:41,492 --> 00:00:45,095
Mswati the Second ruled in the eighteen
hundreds, and he united and

15
00:00:45,115 --> 00:00:46,251
expanded the kingdom.

16
00:00:46,751 --> 00:00:48,086
His people took his name.

17
00:00:48,586 --> 00:00:52,159
A century earlier, a king named Ngwane the
Third had led them across

18
00:00:52,179 --> 00:00:54,557
the Lubombo mountains and founded the
nation.

19
00:00:55,057 --> 00:00:58,521
In nineteen oh three, after the Boer War,
Britain took control.

20
00:00:59,021 --> 00:01:02,331
Independence came on September sixth,
nineteen sixty-eight.

21
00:01:02,831 --> 00:01:06,044
Fifty years later, the king announced the
new name.

22
00:01:06,544 --> 00:01:07,544
Eswatini.

23
00:01:07,814 --> 00:01:09,456
It means land of the Swazis.

24
00:01:09,956 --> 00:01:12,280
He called Swaziland a colonial name.

25
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:15,723
He also said that abroad, people kept
confusing it with Switzerland.

26
00:01:16,223 --> 00:01:18,222
But the name is the easy part.

27
00:01:18,722 --> 00:01:20,621
The hard part is who actually rules.

28
00:01:21,121 --> 00:01:23,978
Sobhuza the Second was crowned in nineteen
twenty-one.

29
00:01:24,478 --> 00:01:28,124
He reigned for more than sixty years, one
of the longest reigns in history.

30
00:01:28,624 --> 00:01:31,829
Then, in nineteen seventy-three, he
cancelled the constitution and

31
00:01:31,849 --> 00:01:33,070
banned political parties.

32
00:01:33,570 --> 00:01:36,303
In their place came a system called
Tinkhundla.

33
00:01:36,803 --> 00:01:40,997
Voters choose people in local districts,
as individuals, never as parties.

34
00:01:41,497 --> 00:01:45,403
His son, Mswati the Third, was crowned in
nineteen eighty-six, at

35
00:01:45,423 --> 00:01:46,423
only eighteen.

36
00:01:46,769 --> 00:01:49,755
Today the king appoints the prime
minister, and he also picks some

37
00:01:49,775 --> 00:01:50,775
of the lawmakers.

38
00:01:51,049 --> 00:01:52,676
And he never rules alone.

39
00:01:53,176 --> 00:01:55,536
Tradition gives Eswatini two rulers.

40
00:01:56,036 --> 00:01:57,534
The king is the Lion.

41
00:01:58,034 --> 00:01:59,975
His mother is the Great She-Elephant.

42
00:02:00,475 --> 00:02:02,148
The Lion leads the government.

43
00:02:02,648 --> 00:02:05,033
The She-Elephant is the spiritual head of
the nation.

44
00:02:05,533 --> 00:02:07,080
Even the capital is split.

45
00:02:07,580 --> 00:02:09,986
Mbabane is the administrative capital.

46
00:02:10,486 --> 00:02:13,136
Lobamba is the royal and legislative
capital.

47
00:02:13,636 --> 00:02:17,488
To feel how this works, watch what happens
each year when the nation

48
00:02:17,508 --> 00:02:18,443
gathers.

49
00:02:18,463 --> 00:02:22,706
People greet each other with sawubona,
which means hello, and answer

50
00:02:22,726 --> 00:02:24,105
yebo, which means yes.

51
00:02:24,605 --> 00:02:28,072
At the table, you eat sishwala, a thick
maize porridge.

52
00:02:28,572 --> 00:02:33,210
And at Mantenga, dancers perform sibhaca,
a stamping dance, twice a day.

53
00:02:33,710 --> 00:02:37,570
But the biggest ceremony comes at the end
of August, or in early September.

54
00:02:38,070 --> 00:02:39,839
It is called Umhlanga, the Reed Dance.

55
00:02:40,339 --> 00:02:45,580
For about eight days, thousands of
unmarried young women walk to the

56
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:47,843
riverbeds and cut tall reeds.

57
00:02:48,343 --> 00:02:51,057
They carry them to the queen mother's
residence, to renew the

58
00:02:51,077 --> 00:02:52,077
windbreak around it.

59
00:02:52,473 --> 00:02:55,628
In its modern form, the ceremony took
shape in the nineteen forties,

60
00:02:55,648 --> 00:02:56,815
under Sobhuza the Second.

61
00:02:57,315 --> 00:03:01,251
A few months later, in December or
January, comes Incwala, the

62
00:03:01,271 --> 00:03:02,611
ceremony of kingship.

63
00:03:03,111 --> 00:03:05,267
And without a king, there is no Incwala.

64
00:03:05,767 --> 00:03:08,002
So how does a country like this earn a
living?

65
00:03:08,502 --> 00:03:09,932
Mostly through its giant neighbor.

66
00:03:10,432 --> 00:03:14,186
South Africa buys most of its exports, and
sells it most of what it imports.

67
00:03:14,686 --> 00:03:18,587
The lilangeni, its currency, is pegged one
to one to the South African rand.

68
00:03:19,087 --> 00:03:22,615
It sells sugar, wood pulp, and the
concentrate used to make soft

69
00:03:22,635 --> 00:03:24,187
drinks, including Coca-Cola.

70
00:03:24,687 --> 00:03:27,910
Eswatini also faced a health crisis.

71
00:03:28,410 --> 00:03:32,353
At its peak, more than one adult in four
had HIV, the highest rate on Earth.

72
00:03:32,854 --> 00:03:36,166
Then the country fought back, and in
twenty twenty it became the

73
00:03:36,186 --> 00:03:39,257
first to reach the United Nations targets,
ten years early.

74
00:03:39,757 --> 00:03:41,823
And the land itself holds a surprise.

75
00:03:42,323 --> 00:03:43,925
White rhinos roam Hlane.

76
00:03:44,425 --> 00:03:46,257
Zebras and hippos share Mlilwane.

77
00:03:46,757 --> 00:03:50,216
And in the Lion Cavern, people were mining
red ochre about

78
00:03:50,236 --> 00:03:54,555
forty-eight thousand years ago, in one of
the oldest mines in the world.

79
00:03:55,055 --> 00:03:56,304
That was Eswatini.

80
00:03:56,804 --> 00:03:58,108
Which country should we visit next?

81
00:03:58,608 --> 00:03:59,923
Tell me in the comments.

